{
  "abstract": "We give improved lower and upper bounds for the universal antiderivative\nlattice governing finite quadratic jet determinants. A finite-depth\napproximation retains integral polynomial coefficients on residue disks\nand every primitive boundary value. We determine its entire first-depth\nvolume, $\\beta_N[1]=N^2/(2p(2p+1))+O_p(N\\log(N+1))$.\nAn integral inverse theorem for tame beta-moment matrices supplies boundary\nextensions. Projecting deeper basis vectors along these extensions\npreserves global independence and the selected boundary volume. Joint\nrow and column potentials bound the remaining constraint minors.\nA truncated triangular change to the deep digit basis gives a second\nextension with only a logarithmic degree buffer. The resulting finite\nlower bounds use explicit floors and piecewise-linear sums. At $p=2$,\nthey give quadratic coefficients $1681/31740$ at depth two and\n$478696811/8992035700=0.05323564396\\ldots$ at depth four, improving\n$(\\sqrt{105}-9)/24$. The same buffered columns force a minimum boundary\ncost inside the complete integer-valued polynomial lattice. This gives\n$\\limsup\\beta_N/N^2\\le1/[2(p+1)(2p-1)]$, with an explicit finite error;\nthe dyadic upper coefficient improves from $469/7992$ to $1/18$.\nAt degree $2^{18}$ our certificates give\n$3640659013\\le\\beta_N\\le3825369961$.\nThe lower construction transfers to the original quadratic determinant\ncontract, adding more than $0.00005157035599$ to its recursive-module\narithmetic comparison with unchanged rank, support, derivative capacity\nand metric. The upper estimate supplies no lower divisibility credit.\nSupporting results locate the earlier recursive construction in the depth\nfiltration and give a clustered Hermite determinant. Exact rational and\nquadratic-field author controls accompany the proofs. These bounds do not prove convergence\nor determine the exact unrestricted coefficient. No improved discriminant\nbound, census, or classification is claimed.",
